# =====================================================================================
# TerminalSchema.manifest - the TERMINAL shape of the twenty-one mapped DotNetNuke
# tables, derived from the legacy upgrade scripts and from nothing this solution emits.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS
#   The integration database is provisioned from Schema/DnnSchema.sql, and that script
#   used to describe itself as having been emitted from the entity model with
#   "dotnet ef dbcontext script". Any comparison of the model against a database built
#   from a model-emitted script compares the model with itself: a model that is
#   internally consistent and uniformly wrong satisfies every such assertion. This file
#   breaks that circle by stating the schema INDEPENDENTLY, so that the model, the
#   provisioning script and the provisioned catalogue are all measured against an
#   authority none of them produced.
#
#   It is not theoretical. Measuring against this manifest for the first time found two
#   drifted declarations in DnnSchema.sql that the circular comparison had passed for
#   as long as it existed:
#     * Roles.PortalID was declared NULL where the terminal schema declares it NOT NULL,
#       so the fixture accepted a role with no owning portal - a row no installation can
#       hold - and two persistence tests asserted behaviour over exactly such a row.
#     * Seven primary keys were provisioned CLUSTERED where the terminal schema and the
#       entity configurations both declare them NONCLUSTERED.
#
# HOW IT WAS DERIVED
#   Primary authority: the 83 versioned upgrade scripts in
#   Website/Providers/DataProviders/SqlDataProvider (00.00.00 .. 04.09.00 - the folder
#   holds 88 *.SqlDataProvider files in all, the other five being the installer, the
#   uninstaller and the fresh-install snapshot named below), replayed in version order.
#   Those 83 scripts carry 68,005 lines, 685 ALTER TABLE statements and 30 DROP TABLE
#   statements, and the chain is destructive rather than additive, so ONLY the cumulative
#   terminal state is meaningful and no single CREATE TABLE may be read as authoritative.
#   The measured extent of that, over these twenty-one tables alone: 15 of them take the
#   terminal shape of at least one column from a script LATER than the one that created
#   the table, and 4 of them - ModuleSettings, Portals, Roles and Users - were destroyed
#   outright and rebuilt through a Tmp_ copy that was renamed back over them. Roles was
#   rebuilt twice, and the two rebuilds disagree about the nullability of PortalID, which
#   is exactly how the drifted declaration named above came to look defensible.
#
#   Every record below therefore cites the LAST statement that established the shape it
#   records, as <script>:<line>, and every one of those 194 citations was re-read to
#   confirm that the cited line does declare that column with that type and width.
#
#   Corroborating authority: DotNetNuke.Schema.SqlDataProvider, the Red Gate SQL Compare
#   snapshot of the 4.x development database that ships in the same folder and that a
#   fresh installation is provisioned from. It is a second legacy artefact produced by a
#   different tool from a different database, and it agrees with the replay on every one
#   of the 194 columns except the eleven listed below, each of which a script LATER than
#   the snapshot explains:
#     DesktopModules.Dependencies, DesktopModules.Permissions   added 04.05.00
#     ModuleControls.SupportsPartialRendering                   added 04.05.00
#     ModulePermission.UserID, TabPermission.UserID             added 04.05.00
#     ModulePermission.RoleID, TabPermission.RoleID             relaxed to NULL 04.05.00
#     ProfilePropertyDefinition.DefaultValue                    retyped ntext  04.05.00
#     Permission.PermissionKey                                  widened to 50  04.06.00
#     Tabs.IsSecure                                             added 04.05.04
#
#   ONE GENUINE DISAGREEMENT between the two authorities is recorded rather than hidden.
#   The snapshot declares Portals.PortalID as IDENTITY(0, 1) while the upgrade chain
#   declares IDENTITY(-1, 1) at 01.00.00:77 and again through the rebuild at
#   01.00.05:1364. The chain is followed, because it is the path this migration binds to
#   and the seed is load-bearing: -1 is simultaneously the first real tenant key and the
#   legacy "absent integer" marker. The consequence for a fresh-install database is
#   documented in MIGRATION_NOTES.md rather than resolved here.
#
# WHAT IS ASSERTED, AND WHAT DELIBERATELY IS NOT
#   Asserted: the table set, every column with its type, declared width, nullability and
#   identity seed, the primary key with its name, ordered columns and clustering, every
#   non-primary index with its name, ordered columns and uniqueness, and every physical
#   foreign key with its name, columns, principal and delete action.
#
#   Not asserted: ORDINAL COLUMN POSITION, because the terminal order depends on which
#   path an installation took - a Tmp_ rebuild reorders the columns it copies, so an
#   upgraded database and a freshly installed one legitimately differ, and the two
#   authorities above differ from each other. Also not asserted: DEFAULT constraints and
#   the WITH NOCHECK and NOT FOR REPLICATION qualifiers, which have no EF Core
#   counterpart, and NUMERIC PRECISION, which for the only non-integer numeric type in
#   this set - money - is fixed by the type name that IS asserted.
#
# RECORD GRAMMAR
#   Fields are separated by "|" and trimmed; "-" means "none". A line whose first
#   non-space character is "#" is a comment. Field one selects the record:
#     TABLE      | table | primary key name | key columns | CLUSTERED-ness | provenance
#     COLUMN     | table | column | type | max length | nullability | identity | provenance
#     INDEX      | name | table | columns | UNIQUE or INDEX | provenance
#     FOREIGNKEY | name | table | columns | principal | principal columns | delete | provenance
#
#   Names carry the legacy spelling exactly, upper and lower case included. The legacy
#   scripts template every object name as {databaseOwner}{objectQualifier}Name; this
#   installation declares databaseOwner="dbo" and objectQualifier="" (release.config
#   L354-L355), so the names here are the un-prefixed forms that resolves to.
#
# THIS FILE IS AN ORACLE AND MUST NEVER BE EDITED TO MAKE A TEST PASS. If a comparison
# against it fails, either the model or DnnSchema.sql has drifted, and the fix belongs
# there. A change here is legitimate only when a cited script line is shown to say
# something other than what the record claims.
# =====================================================================================

